Subject: Why have children?

In #93-12-924 carlf@media.mit.edu (Carl Feynman) writes:
>If it turns out that a gene can predispose to homosexuality, people will
>put lots of effort into making sure that gene doesn't get into their kids. 
>And you don't have to be homophobic to want to do that, though that
>certainly helps.  I don't think I would want my kids to part of a despised
>minority, and less likely to produce grandchildren.

Homosexuals can have children, and many would choose to have homosexual
children. If parents were very concerned about their children being
part of a despised minority, why did Jews continue to raise their
children as Jews for millenia? I think parents are much more interested
in having children like themselves than having children that are
well-adjusted, not despised, or intelligent.

As another childless acquaintance put it, 'having kids is just a big
ego trip.'

I'm not accusing all breeders (referring to those who have chilren, not
heterosexuals) of being jerks -- David Friedman's recent post about why
one would have children made some excellent points. If I ever have
children, feel free to beat me senseless with this post (in a VR of
course :).

>An interseting question is what will happen if an edited child costs
>$20,000 up front to produce.  This would be enough that poor people would
>not be able to afford it.  Then we would have a self-perpetuating
>hereditary upper class that really was superior.

Perhaps I'm suffering from futurapture, but I doubt the human race as
we know it will be around enough generations for such a class to
develop (doesn't mean that neophobes won't write scare pieces about the
possibility though) or that the cost would stay high over enough
generations.

