Date: Tue, 28 Dec 1993 22:38:20 -0800
From: jamie@netcom.com (Jamie Dinkelacker)
Subject: IQ TESTS & MENSA

*
I have empathy with (and will take certain liberties paraphrasing) Stuart
Card's remarks about being raised in a rural area where the youth know
about milking machines (and fixing tractors) and the adults see honest
questions as only coming from a smartass kid. Yep, I was raised in that
hell as well. I got slapped around as a kid for questioning Holy Mother The
Church and the wisdom of Clan Moran (my family --  primarily Irish descent,
my Dutch/German surname notwithstanding). 

We had no Mensa in my rural area, and my first contact was when I went to
college. Some Mensans (?) approached me due to entrance exam results and
scholarship awards, but the organization's members made me uncomfortable
for several reasons: the guys I knew who were members tended to be devoid
of social skills, they tended to be condescending towards others, and none
had an attractive girlfriend (if he had a romantic companion at all). As a
young man with a very strong liking for the pleasures of female
companionship, such a club wasn't to be happy hunting grounds. Plus, I
presumed that the guys in the "club" (or their equivalent replacements)
would still be there in the future, probably speculating on the same ol'
stuff: no need to hurry or pay present attention.

As a young upstart, I had much more of an "action" orientation. I was far
more interested in publicly performing as a musician (great way to meet
women and make money), understanding media, learning organizational theory,
studying effective management practices, and trying to find people of
"real-world" accomplishment who would mentor me. So, I don't know much
about Mensa, other than I was quite sure that the members (in Troy NY in
the late '60s) probably wouldn't be fun to be around for someone with my
personality.

But underneath all this is an interesting question: how relevant is
"intelligence" as measured by a neat little test to success in life,
especially for creating the dyanmic situations that many Extropians would
like to bring about? 

Would a "smartness" measure contribute to a more Extropian future? Does it
matter? If so, to whom? For what purpose?

