Subject: MORALS: Not Mere Aesthetics

Geoff Dale writes: <First of all, extropianism is a new movement.>

Perhaps under this name.  It's themes have been kicking around for a very long
time:  immortality, extension of the self's abilities, progress, expansion,
order, optimism, etc.  It's an attempt to kickstart Western civilization using
good old rationality (a Western invention), good old values of rugged
individualism and boundless projection of the ego.  I can't see much new in it
other than a repackaging job.  I identify strongly with it, I think it has great
potential if it catches hold in large percentages of Western civilization (or
anywhere else for that matter, it's just that it's a Western offshoot making it
easier to catch hold in the West where the moral values are closely aligned).
If it is just another Utopian theoretical fringe group not reaching out to
others, it will not have lasting impact.  If it's right, it will supplant
existing memes on a large scale, if not, maybe something else will spin off it.
I personally hope it becomes more than a secret society of dogmatic elites, a
sort of lodge, like BPOE; Benevolent and Protective Order of the Extropians.

Geoff: <Democratic institutions are not part of the extropian principles, try
Spontaneous Order, which is essentially a form of anarchy. That, combined with
free markets, makes us anarchocapitalists.>

Spontaneous Order in society comes from putting energy into creating coherent
social bonds based on consensual rationality and consensual morality.  No
Utopian theories, please, let's look at what has worked in the past and apply it
to the future.  If you don't like the phrase "democratic institutions" then use
"self-determination".  The Extropian Principles are incomplete, the Extropian
Philosophy is incomplete, yet you cast them as dogma.

Anybody can call themselves an amoralist, that doesn't make them so.  Nietzsche
could write all he wanted to about "beyond good and evil" but he couldn't purge
himself of his private first-person subjective values or his second-person
consensual social values.  No one can, it is part of us.  No one is rational
without assigning value to fact.  Denial of values is denial of reality.  All
values are moral values, assignment of value implies that one choice is better
than another choice.  This fact that one choice is better than another choice
points directly to the concept of "good" as a moral absolute.  There is no
avoidance of "good", or no choice but random choice would be made.  People who
profess amorality or moral relativism are in denial of reality or deliberately
deceiving others to promote alternate moral values.

Geoff: <You can discuss it. You just get nowhere using it instead of evidence.>

Evidence is laden with value, along with fact.

<Social values over individual liberties is a "leftist, entropic
death-worshipper" meme>

Individual liberties cannot exist without underlying coherent extropic social
values.  I know people don't like to use the word "social", but there it is.

Geoff: <From the welcome message: Extropians are rational individualists, living
by their own judgment, making reflective, informed choices, profiting from both
success and shortcoming.>

Judgment is moral judgment.  Choices are value laden.  Success must be examined
carefully, rationally for clues as to what will work in the future and not just
as a support for a pre-existing theory.  Hence, a review of historical periods
of extropic advance.

Geoff: <I, as an extropian, hereby deny the existence of good and evil.  Good
and evil are bogeymen, invented by the control freaks of religion, to scare the
masses into their statist clutches.>

Denying it doesn't make it go away.  Let's not denigrate "control freaks", I've
seen people, posting on this list, setting themselves up as judges of what
passes dogmatic review and what doesn't.  People who live in glass houses
shouldn't throw stones.

Geoff: <I pronounce something as irrational when I've analyzed a concept and
found that it fails to have logical or scientific basis.>

You analyze the concept using value and judgment, both moral in nature, both
relating to the concept of "good" logic or evidence or "bad" logic or evidence.
Science is open to consensual social values for definition as is logic.
Inductive logic is open-ended and self-referential calling for value judgments.
Pronouncing something as rational, scientific or logical uses second-person
social values.

Geoff: <The ability to separate the rational and the irrational is one of the
things that sets the extropians above most folks, IMEO.>

This separation is socially derived second-person and is of the utmost
importance to everyone, not just Extropians.  The rules of what rational is, and
what irrational is, are not cast in concrete here or anywhere else.

Geoff: <She <Romana> and I claim to have studied the Extropian Principles, read
many of the items on the reading list, read "Extropy: the Journal of
Transhumanist Thought", spoken to extropians and joined the Extropy Institute.
Because of this, we claim to have a good idea of what extropianism is and what
extropian's think.>

Count me in!  I've done all that, too.  Let's not have holier-than-thou stuff in
this group.

Geoff: <The extropians here don't seem to be swallowing your brand of
extropianism. You are not where you think you are, and we are not who you think
we are. I suggest that you have another look at the situation before you
continue this discussion.>

Let's see, 2 out of 344 makes a majority?  What is it, two blackballs and you're
out?  Or are some people's blackballs more equal than others?  This sentence
strikes me as the Argument From Intimidation and is usually resorted to when
someone is overcome by a desire to purify ideas that deviate from dogma.  I
would thank those who offered private support.  If you are upset with me because
you don't like my characterization of the MONDO2000 editors because you are
working with them, I understand your feelings.  Considering the content of the
posts I have seen from their editors, I am suspicious of MONDO2000's interest in
Extropians.  It is possible they could simply view us as an addition to their
Freak Show Pantheon they look to for entertainment while they wait to die.  Of
course, as in Hollywood, any publicity we can get, good or bad, is still
publicity.

