Duncan Frissell writes:

> Kennita has correctly identified Tim's role as that of the warrior.  As I 
> read her description, above, I was reminded of Niven and Pournelle's book
> "Legacy of Herelot."  It deals with the conflict between the social and 
> the military virtues in the context of a somewhat voluntary community.  
> Well worth the read.
> 

Just so you all know, I don't claim to be a warrior. My draft lottery
number in 1971 was 181, so I dropped my student deferment (I'll bet
10% of you reading this have no idea at all what I'm talking about
here) and "exposed" myself to being drafted....no risk to me, with a
number that high.

So, I safely avoided Vietnam and the regimentation of military life.
And I've never been mugged...it may help that I'm 6'2" and 210 pounds,
as mugger often pick smaller targets and folks less apparently likely
to be carrying a gun or knife.

But I can't claim to be a warrior, as I haven't been "tested." Maybe
I'll freeze up. Maybe I'll panic. I _hope_ that my years of
preparation will allow me to defend myself in the best way possible.

One thing's for sure, I think. And that's that I don't believe any
mystical mumbo-jumbo about the sanctity of human life. We're
biological machines, we live, we die. A person who dies at some age is
just dead....mysticism about "not having lived" or "not fulfilling his
potential" is nonsense (else we wouldn't allow abortions either).

My family is important to me, my friends are important to me, my cats
are important to me, and so on. Emphasis on "my." This is really why
people don't like to see people die---when there's a real connection.

So, I can be "heartless" when it comes to the death and suffering of
complete strangers and still seek to protect/help my friends and
family. This is neither moral nor immoral nor amoral...it is just the
way we evolved. No logical system can ever say that saving _my_ family
is somehow more justified than saving others....and yet we know that
this is what people do.

The "market" focus of Extropians provides the key to understanding
this: we protect, defend, support, and cherish the things close to us,
not distant abstractions. Morality is in a sense aesthetics.

(To be banal about it, I just had pork chops tonight---sorry
Vegans!---and yet I would barf if I were offered roast dog or grilled
cat. We consider eating pigs OK but eating cats and dogs is not OK.
Think about the morality of this. Oh, and in _my_ PPL I would
certainly take steps to stop others from raising and eating dogs and
cats. Morality is aesthetics---and what you're prepared to confront.)

Despite the fears of a few on this List--maybe just one person--I can
disagree with someone, or be oblivious to their "issues," without
wanting to destroy them.

Folks I don't want to deal with, I simply avoid. No need to "work
things out," to "get the issues on the table," to "run our processes,"
or any other psychobabble. Just avoidance.

A typical male behavior, say the experts. A survival behavior, say the
evolutionary biologists. A way to simplify my life, say I.

And it's pretty telling that the List justice system is essentially an
implementation of this avoidance strategy, via ::excludes, rather than
a "family counseling" and "therapy" system.

Not an accident.

--Tim May


