From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Speed of evolution/selection of traits in humans...

[David Friedman elaborates on my discussion of measurements of genetic
versus environmental contributions to intelligence.]

I agree completely with Friedman's points.  Indeed, in the experiments I
cited, there are potential effects from selection that could alter the
result in either direction; thus, the agencies placing children for adoption
almost certainly excluded certain kinds of family environments (such as
substantial poverty, single-parent families, and families that did not
want another child), and the fact that certain children were up for adoption
in the first place may reflect that their biological parents were not
intelligent enough to avoid unwanted pregnancies.

The arguments about what the statistics I cited mean can go on and on; I
personally am inclined to view the result as what I might term "exploratory
science", in which one is concerned merely with verifying that a phenomenon
exists -- quantitative measurements can come later.

                                             -- Jay Freeman

