Subject: Extropially Correct Welfare

Jay Freeman writes:

>   Yup, and this is also an issue for several youngster-benefit programs that
> seem to me to have high potential payback, such as ones dealing with health
> and nutrition all the way back to pre-natal.

Sure, nutrition, vaccinations, all that stuff is important for
children. Sure, if children don't get some of these things the cost
down the line _to someone_ (maybe the child, maybe the parents...)
could be higher than if they'd gotten the things before.

This situation happens all the time. Maybe if nobody pays me law
school tuition I'll be come a thief instead. Maybe if nobody pays for
my new start up company to get going, I'll have to take a low-paying
job and thus society will ultimately pay for this oversight. Such
things never end.

If my neighbor has children, I feel no obligation to help feed,
shelter, clothe, educate, or vaccinate his children. (If I am to be
near them, though, I may take steps to make sure _I_ am vaccinated!)
If he cannot afford these things, perhaps some charity will help. If
he _chooses_ not to afford these things, then too bad. If we use the
power of the State to coerce transfers from me to him, then he will
come to realize very quickly that he can use _his_ money to buy beer
and drugs and use _my_ money to buy the necessitities (and maybe some
more beer, too).

This is the practical reason I don't let arguments about the "cheaper
in the long run to do X" sway me. Prenatal care is indeed cheap. We
often hear that $100 spent on sonograms, vaccinations, routine
check-ups will save tens of thousands of dollars in the long run.

I agree. But, I ask, who cannot find $100 in this day and age? What
pregant mother cannot get her husband to earn an extra $100 by working
some overtime, by doing some part-time jobs?

(Oh, you say they're not married, that she has no visible means of
support, that her pimp has abandoned her. Then by all means give here
none of my money.)

> prompted the Squirrel's original challenge.  For if an Extropianly correct
> free-market economy cannot provide some way for entrepreneurs to reap some
> of the probable trillions of dollars of economic output that education of
> children has produced, then perhaps one should wonder whether the free
> market is indeed the end-point in the search for forms of political economy
> that encourage human development.
> 
>   Right?

Sorry, Jay, wrong. In a true free market, it would be up to _parents_
to contract for schooling, not for entrepreneurs to somehow tap into
future productivity. Much the way most private schools and nearly all
colleges, private or state, work. 

Parents are the ones to make this asessment of future economic output
of their children and thus to contract in the present for teachers and
schools. The teachers and schools care to be good teachers because
that directly affects the market rate for their services.

>   [See what I meant about "outlier position on controversial topics"?]
> 
>                                      -- Jay Freeman, First Extropian Squirrel

Not nearly as outlying as my views, I suspect. For example, in the
school example, a true free market would support the right of
contract, wherein I could make a deal to pay of the education of, say,
some ghetto kids and they would then be contractually bound to work
for me for some number of years.

That this is not allowed--some nonsense about "indentured
servitude"--is indicative that we are not at all in a free market.

(Ironically, the government bans these education-for-work deals,
except for itself. You all know what I'm referring to.)

--Tim May



